=> Ways to Read Java Files

    -> Java provides multiple built-in APIs to read files.

    -> Common approaches:
        1. FileReader
        2. BufferedReader
        3. Files.readString()

    -> All three belong to the standard Java API.

    -> They can read the contents of a .java file without compiling or executing its source code.

    -> Important:
        -> Reading Java source code and understanding Java source code are two different operations.

        -> Reading:
            -> Retrieves file contents as characters or text.

        -> Analyzing:
            -> Identifies classes, methods, fields, annotations, and other Java structures.


=> FileReader

    -> Package:
        -> java.io.FileReader

    -> Definition:
        -> FileReader is a Java class used to read character data from a file.

    -> Working:
        -> Opens a file.
        -> Reads characters using read().
        -> read() returns the next character as an integer.
        -> Returns -1 when the end of the file is reached.

    -> Characteristics:
        -> Suitable for reading text files.
        -> Can read one character at a time.
        -> Does not understand Java syntax.
        -> IOException must be handled or declared.
        -> try-with-resources automatically closes the reader.

    -> Example: FileReaderExample.java

    
    -> Explanation:
        -> FileReader opens BankService.java.
        -> read() retrieves characters sequentially.
        -> (char) converts the returned integer into a character.
        -> System.out.print() displays each character.
        -> When read() returns -1, reading stops.

    -> Limitations:
        -> Reading character by character is inconvenient
           for processing complete lines.
        -> Does not identify class or method declarations.
        -> Does not identify field names or annotations.
        -> Does not create an AST.


=> BufferedReader

    -> Package:
        -> java.io.BufferedReader

    -> Definition:
        -> BufferedReader reads character data efficiently by using an internal buffer.

    -> Working:
        -> Reads data from another Reader.
        -> Uses a buffer to reduce underlying read operations.
        -> Provides readLine() for reading complete lines.
        -> readLine() returns null at the end of the file.

    -> Characteristics:
        -> Suitable for line-by-line processing.
        -> Usually more convenient than FileReader alone.
        -> Buffer size can be configured.
        -> Does not understand Java syntax.

    -> Example: BufferReaderExample.java

    
    -> Explanation:
        -> FileReader reads character data from the file.
        -> BufferedReader buffers that character data.
        -> readLine() retrieves one complete line at a time.
        -> Each line is stored in a String variable.
        -> The loop continues until readLine() returns null.

    -> Limitations:
        -> Understands lines, not Java declarations.
        -> Cannot inherently distinguish a method declaration from a method call.
        -> Cannot automatically identify annotations.
        -> Cannot create an AST.

    -> Important:
        -> BufferedReader improves text-reading convenience and efficiency.
        -> It does not provide Java language awareness.


=> Files.readString()

    -> Package:
        -> java.nio.file.Files

    -> Introduced:
        -> Java 11

    -> Definition:
        -> Files.readString() is a utility method that reads a complete file into a String.

    -> Working:
        -> Accepts a Path representing the file.
        -> Reads the file contents.
        -> Converts the contents into a String.
        -> Returns the complete text.

    -> Characteristics:
        -> Short and readable implementation.
        -> No explicit Reader object required.
        -> Uses UTF-8 by default.
        -> Suitable for reasonably sized text files.
        -> Loads the complete file content into memory.

    -> Example: FilesReadStringExample.java

    
    -> Explanation:
        -> Path.of() represents the file path.
        -> Files.readString() reads the complete file.
        -> The file content is stored in sourceCode.
        -> System.out.println() prints the source code.

    -> Limitations:
        -> Entire file content is loaded into memory.
        -> May be unsuitable for very large files.
        -> Returns plain text, not structured Java nodes.
        -> Does not identify classes, methods, or annotations.
        -> Does not create an AST.



=> Limitations of Traditional File Reading

    -> FileReader, BufferedReader, and Files.readString() are designed to read file contents.

    -> They do not automatically parse Java grammar.

    -> Problem 1: Class Identification
        -> Cannot directly identify which text represents a Java class declaration.

    -> Problem 2: Method Identification
        -> Cannot directly distinguish between:
            -> MethodDeclaration
            -> MethodCallExpr
            -> ConstructorDeclaration

    -> Problem 3: Annotation Identification
        -> Cannot directly associate annotations with
           their corresponding Java declarations.

    -> Problem 4: Nested Code Structure
        -> Cannot automatically determine whether a method
           belongs to a class, nested class, or another type.

    -> Problem 5: Comments and String Literals
        -> Simple text searches may mistake code written
           inside comments or strings for actual Java code.

    -> Problem 6: Manual Parsing Complexity
        -> Developers would have to implement custom logic.
        -> Such logic must handle Java syntax variations.
        -> Regular expressions alone are not a reliable
           replacement for a complete Java parser.

    -> Important:
        -> These APIs can still be used as part of a scanner.
        -> Their limitation is that they do not provide
           language-aware analysis automatically.


=> JavaParser — Solution to Traditional File Reading Limitations

    -> Definition:
        -> JavaParser is an open-source Java library.
        -> It parses Java source code into a structured representation called an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST).

    -> Purpose:
        -> Read and analyze Java source code.
        -> Identify Java language constructs.
        -> Navigate source-code structures.
        -> Support source-code transformation.

    -> JavaParser can identify:
        -> Class declarations.
        -> Interface declarations.
        -> Method declarations.
        -> Constructor declarations.
        -> Field declarations.
        -> Annotations.
        -> Method calls.
        -> Expressions and statements.

    -> Basic Working Flow:

        Java Source File (.java)
                  |
                  v
              JavaParser
                  |
                  v
          Lexical Analysis
                  |
                  v
           Syntax Analysis
                  |
                  v
         Abstract Syntax Tree
                  |
                  v
           Inspect AST Nodes
                  |
                  v
          Extract Information

    -> Important:
        -> JavaParser does not execute source code.
        -> It does not require successful compilation to recognize syntactically valid Java structures.
        -> Basic parsing does not guarantee semantic validity.
        -> Symbol Solver can additionally resolve references when configured with the required type information.

    -> Example: JavaParserExample.java

    
    -> Explanation:
        -> StaticJavaParser.parse() parses the Java file.

        -> CompilationUnit represents the parsed
           Java source file.

        -> findAll(ClassOrInterfaceDeclaration.class)
           finds class and interface declarations.

        -> findAll(MethodDeclaration.class)
           finds method declarations.

        -> getNameAsString() retrieves the declaration name.

    -> Advantages for a Java Scanning Project:
        -> Structured analysis instead of plain-text searching.
        -> More reliable handling of Java syntax.
        -> Supports complex and nested Java structures.
        -> AST nodes can be navigated programmatically.
        -> Reduces the need for custom parsing logic.


=> Final Conclusion

    -> FileReader:
        -> Used for character-based file reading.

    -> BufferedReader:
        -> Used for efficient buffered text reading, especially line-by-line processing.

    -> Files.readString():
        -> Used to read an entire text file into a String.

    -> JavaParser:
        -> Used to parse and structurally analyze Java code.

    -> Key Concept:
        -> Traditional Java file-reading APIs provide text.
        -> JavaParser provides a structured representation
           of Java source code.

    -> For Our Java Scanning Project:
        -> Reading files is only the starting point.
        -> JavaParser helps us understand their structure.
        -> AST navigation helps us extract required information.

